      "question_id": "xdr-001",
      "question": "A processor in Germany discovers a personal data breach affecting EU residents. How quickly must we notify, whom exactly, and what does our internal policy require beyond the statutory minimum?",
      "answer_type": "cross_document",
      "answer_text": "A processor in Germany must notify the controller without undue delay after becoming aware of a personal data breach [S2][S3]. According to the statutory minimum, the notification to the supervisory authority should be made without undue delay and, where feasible, not later than 72 hours after having become aware of it, unless the personal data breach is unlikely to result in a risk to the legally protected interests of natural persons [S3].\n\nInternally, the processor must notify the Office of the Data Protection Officer directly [S1]. The internal policy requires that this notification be made within four hours of detection, even if the facts are incomplete or the incident may turn out to be a false alarm [S1][S2][S3].\n\nThe notification to the controller and the supervisory authority should include at least the following information:\n1. A description of the nature of the personal data breach, including where possible, the categories and approximate number of data subjects concerned and the categories and approximate number of personal data records concerned [S2][S3].\n2. The name and contact details of the data protection officer or other contact point where more information can be obtained [S2][S3].\n3. A description of the likely consequences of the personal data breach [S2][S3].\n\nThe internal policy also emphasizes that communications with the public and press must be approved by the Data Protection Officer before issuance [S1].",
