      "question": "Can a regulator force us to replace our data protection officer, and who appoints ours in the first place?",
      "answer_type": "cross_document",
      "answer_text": "According to the national law provided [S3], a data protection officer's employment can be terminated only under specific conditions and not by a regulator's direct order. The data protection officer can be dismissed only if there are just cause to terminate without notice, and even then, the officer cannot be terminated for a year following the end of their appointment unless just cause exists [S3][4].\n\nThe appointment of the data protection officer in the context of a public body is typically done by the public body itself [S3][4]. For a private company, the appointment is made by the company, as stated in the company policy [S2].",
